# Welcome to Markdown Viewer 3.10.4

_Released October 9, 2026_

This release makes Markdown authoring faster from the keyboard, preserves the right content and formatting when you copy, and strengthens navigation and fullscreen behavior across desktop and mobile browsers.

Everything here ships with the web, PWA, Docker, and desktop editions. **Happy coding!**

[View release on GitHub](https://github.com/ThisIs-Developer/Markdown-Viewer/releases/tag/v3.10.4) [Open the full changelog](https://github.com/ThisIs-Developer/Markdown-Viewer/blob/main/CHANGELOG.md)

---

## Highlights

- **Insert blocks without leaving the Editor.** Type `/` at the start of a line, search common Markdown commands, and run one with the keyboard or pointer.
- **Copy the format you intend.** Copy Markdown always returns the full raw document, while Preview Copy supplies sanitized rich HTML plus a readable plain-text alternative.
- **Select confidently on desktop and touch.** Select All stays inside the focused Editor or Preview, works in either document pane, and keeps touch selections available when menus close or reopen.
- **Navigate without layout jumps.** Page Up/Down no longer shifts the workspace sideways and keeps native caret movement, Shift selection, smooth paging, reduced motion, and synchronized panes aligned.
- **Use fullscreen where the browser allows it.** Older Safari APIs are supported, while browsers without page fullscreen receive a clearly named Expanded view with a dependable exit path.

> [!TIP]
> Place the cursor at the beginning of an empty or existing Editor line and type `/`. Filter for a heading, list, quote, code block, table, alert, diagram, image, or another block, then press **Enter**.

## Write faster with slash commands

The new **Insert block** menu opens for a literal `/` at column zero. Keep typing to filter commands, move with the arrow keys, press **Enter** to run the current command, or press **Escape** to close. Mouse and touch selection work too.

The menu includes headings, paragraphs, bulleted and numbered lists, task lists, quotes, horizontal rules, fenced code, terminal blocks, math, links, references, date/time, alerts, tables, diagrams, and images/media. Commands that need more detail open the same focused chooser used by the formatting toolbar.

Normal Markdown stays normal: slashes after indentation or existing text, inside URLs, or during IME composition do not open the menu. The menu follows the active primary or secondary Editor, closes when its document or focus context changes, positions for RTL text, and exposes accessible listbox state.

## Copy and select the right document

**Copy Markdown** in the navbar or mobile menu, plus **Ctrl/Cmd+Shift+C**, now always copies the complete raw source of the intended document in Editor, Preview, or Split mode. In the two-Document view, the last-focused Editor or Preview remains the target even after clicking the toolbar.

Ordinary Editor Copy still returns selected raw Markdown. Preview Copy now writes sanitized HTML and plain text, retaining supported headings, emphasis, links, lists, tables, and code when pasted into rich-text applications such as Google Docs. Partial selections and native browser Copy use the same rich-content path, while Preview controls stay out of the result. Browsers without asynchronous rich clipboard support use a copy-event fallback and then plain text when necessary.

**Select All** and **Ctrl/Cmd+A** are scoped to the focused pane, including secondary and read-only documents. Mouse and keyboard menus close and return focus after selection; touch menus keep Copy available. Dismissing a touch menu preserves the selection, tapping highlighted text reopens it, swipes continue to scroll, and selected text no longer starts an unwanted native drag.

## Dependable navigation and fullscreen

Closed Settings menus no longer add hidden horizontal overflow, fixing the Edge case where Page Up/Down shifted the workspace left and left a blank strip. Paging keeps the browser's caret and Shift-selection semantics. When a browser jumps instantly, Markdown Viewer animates that same destination without interrupting native smooth scrolling; reduced-motion preferences, the secondary Editor, and synchronized panes are respected.

Fullscreen uses the standard API where available and older Safari's prefixed API when required. Requests and exits wait for completion, browser-initiated exits stay synchronized with the button, overlapping requests are prevented, and failed exits remain retryable. The exit control remains usable in Release Notes and after every document tab is closed.

When native page fullscreen is missing or denied, including on iPhone Safari, **Expanded view** hides the app header and fits the workspace to the visible viewport while explaining that browser controls remain visible. Escape closes dialogs first, then exits the expanded layout; printing and document contents are unchanged.

## Explorer, tabs, and desktop polish

Document titles, folder names, and Move-dialog paths now use natural numeric ordering, so `Untitled 2` appears before `Untitled 10`. Workspace rows use the bundled briefcase-business icon consistently across collapsed, expanded, and drag-expanded states.

Pressing a mouse's scroll-wheel button on a tab now closes that tab like a browser tab. It does not activate an inactive tab first, delete its saved Workspace document, or interfere with ordinary wheel scrolling, left-click activation, and right-click menus.

Windows executables and the Neutralino desktop window now use the Markdown Viewer artwork instead of the default runtime icon. The shared slash, sorting, tab, navigation, fullscreen, clipboard, selection, and Workspace-icon changes are synchronized in the prepared desktop resources.

## Quality and compatibility

Focused regression coverage now exercises slash-command lifecycle and accessibility, natural ordering, middle-click behavior, Page Up/Down and synchronized scrolling, Safari and fallback fullscreen paths, rich and plain clipboard writes, pane-scoped selection, touch-menu reopening, drag prevention, read-only documents, and two-Document targeting. All 15 locale catalogs retain matching keys and translated release-note labels.

